  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 5,
    "lesson_title": "Checking Initial and Final Behavior from a Transform",
    "lesson_core_move": "Apply the initial- and final-value theorems to a one-sided Laplace transform $X(s)$ by verifying pole conditions and using transform-domain limits to check starting and steady-state behavior.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Checking Initial and Final Behavior from a Transform",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Checking Initial and Final Behavior from a Transform",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Different transforms can look similar, but behavior checks fail if the theorem conditions are not verified first. In this section, you will use one-sided limits as a diagnostic layer: the checks for $x(0^+)$ and $x(\\infty)$ prevent false conclusions before any graphing or substitution-heavy arithmetic.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the initial value, use the one-sided initial-value rule and a minimal algebra step. Start from the transform expression, form $sX(s)$, then take the $s\\to\\infty$ limit while keeping track of the highest-order terms.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(0^+) = \\lim_{s\\to\\infty} sX(s)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the final value, first confirm the pole condition on $sX(s)$, then use the one-sided limit. If the poles of $sX(s)$ are not all in the left half-plane (except the required simple pole at $s=0$), do not use the formula, even if the arithmetic is easy.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\infty)=\\lim_{s\\to 0} sX(s), \\text{valid only when poles of } sX(s) \\text{ are in } \\Re\\{s\\}<0\\text{ (except one at } s=0\\text{)}.",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use this checklist: identify which limit you need, check the theorem validity condition, evaluate the required transform-domain limit, and record the result. Next sections apply this same move to a concrete initial-value example, then a final-value example, then a rejection case.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Checking Initial and Final Behavior from a Transform",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Checking the Initial Value from $X(s)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find $x(0^+)$ for $X(s)=\\frac{3s+4}{s(s+2)}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is to recover the initial time value directly from a one-sided transform, using the initial-value rule from the prior tutorial. For this section, the workflow is the same as the base move: multiply by $s$, then evaluate the $s\\to\\infty$ limit.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the initial-value theorem in symbolic form and rewrite the expression before limiting:",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(0^+)=\\lim_{s\\to\\infty}sX(s)=\\lim_{s\\to\\infty}\\frac{3s+4}{s+2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Take the limit by keeping the highest-order terms: $(3s+4)/(s+2) \\to 3s/s=3$ as $s\\to\\infty$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(0^+)=3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Result: $x(0^+)=3$. This limit is valid in this example because it is a causal one-sided transform, so the rule applies directly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Checking Initial and Final Behavior from a Transform",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Checking the Final Value When Pole Conditions Hold",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $X(s)=\\frac{4}{s(s+3)}$, verify pole conditions for $sX(s)$ and compute $x(\\infty)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a final-value request from a one-sided transform, so the workflow must start by checking validity before arithmetic. For this step, the governing rule is: form $sX(s)$, verify its poles satisfy the stability condition, then evaluate the final-value limit.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the final-value relation and rewrite the expression to isolate the pole structure:",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\infty)=\\lim_{s\\to 0}sX(s),\\quad X(s)=\\frac{4}{s(s+3)} \\Rightarrow sX(s)=\\frac{4}{s+3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now list poles of $sX(s)=\\frac{4}{s+3}$: the only pole is at $s=-3$, which is strictly in the left half-plane, so the final-value theorem is applicable here.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\infty)=\\lim_{s\\to 0}\\frac{4}{s+3}=\\frac{4}{3}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Final result: the transform satisfies the pole check, so the one-sided final value is $x(\\infty)=\\frac{4}{3}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Checking Initial and Final Behavior from a Transform",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Recognizing When the Final Value Theorem Does Not Apply",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Determine if final-value theorem applies to $X(s)=\\frac{1}{s(s^2+4)}$ and explain the result before attempting a limit.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a requested final-value check, so apply the same workflow as before but stop at the validity gate: form $sX(s)$, factor poles, and verify the allowed pole region before any substitution.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "sX(s)=\\frac{1}{s^2+4}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now check poles of $\\frac{1}{s^2+4}$. They are at $s=\\pm 2j$, both on the imaginary axis, and the validity rule for the one-sided final-value theorem requires poles of $sX(s)$ to be in $\\Re\\{s\\}<0$ except possibly one at $s=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since this condition is violated, do not apply $x(\\infty)=\\lim_{s\\to0}sX(s)$. The theorem is not applicable here for final value in this form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A safe next step is to obtain $x(t)$ by inverse Laplace or perform a separate long-term analysis of the reconstructed signal before claiming a steady-state value.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Checking Initial and Final Behavior from a Transform",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Checking a Reconstructed Signal with Initial and Final Limits",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $X(s)=\\frac{2}{s}-\\frac{1}{s+1}+\\frac{3}{s+2}$, compute $x(0^+)$ and $x(\\infty)$ and verify them against $x(t)=2- e^{-t}+3e^{-2t}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a consistency check for a reconstructed signal, so this step combines both initial- and final-value rules on the same $X(s)$. First compute the limits in the transform domain, then compare them with the values from the provided $x(t)$ form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "sX(s)=2-\\frac{s}{s+1}+\\frac{3s}{s+2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Compute the initial limit from the one-sided rule:",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(0^+)=\\lim_{s\\to\\infty}sX(s)=\\lim_{s\\to\\infty}\\left(2-\\frac{s}{s+1}+\\frac{3s}{s+2}\\right)=4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "sX(s)=\\frac{2(s+1)(s+2)-s(s+2)+3s(s+1)}{s(s+1)(s+2)}=\\frac{3s^2+6s+2}{s(s+1)(s+2)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the final-value check, verify poles of $sX(s)$: the denominator gives poles at $s=0,-1,-2$. All are allowable for the one-sided final-value condition (LHP poles only, with at most one simple pole at $s=0$), so the theorem can be used.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(\\infty)=\\lim_{s\\to0}sX(s)=\\lim_{s\\to0}\\left(2-\\frac{s}{s+1}+\\frac{3s}{s+2}\\right)=2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Evaluate the reconstructed signal: $x(0^+)=2-1+3=4$ and $x(\\infty)=2-0+0=2$. These match $x(0^+)=4$ and $x(\\infty)=2$ from transform limits, so the reconstruction is behaviorally consistent.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
